{
  "passages": [
    {
      "reference": "1",
      "verdict": "blocked",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": true,
        "no_added_claims": true,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "lexical_fidelity": false,
        "uncertainties_disclosed": false
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "τῷ κυριῳ καὶ ἐπισκόπῳ τῆς ἁγίας ἐκκλησίας, ὑπὸ τοῦ τῶν ὅλων πατρὸς, διὰ Ιησοῦ Χριστοῦ",
          "english_quote": "the lord and bishop of the holy church: peace always from the Father of all, through Jesus Christ.",
          "issue": "Two apparent orthographic defects are unreported: the supplied “κυριῳ” lacks its expected accent, and “Ιησοῦ” lacks its expected initial breathing. These do not make the intended words unintelligible, but their normalization should not be silent.",
          "suggested_revision": "Disclose the supplied spellings separately from the expected normalized forms “κυρίῳ” and “Ἰησοῦ”. Do not present those normalized forms as verified readings of the cited print.",
          "severity": "minor"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "ὑπὸ τοῦ τῶν ὅλων πατρὸς, διὰ Ιησοῦ Χριστοῦ, ἐν εἰρήνῃ πάντοτε",
          "english_quote": "peace always from the Father of all, through Jesus Christ.",
          "issue": "The English adopts an elliptical wish for peace, converting “ἐν εἰρήνῃ” into the thing wished and interpreting “ὑπό” as the source of that peace. This is a possible interpretation, but the Greek expressly has “by/under,” “through,” and “in peace,” without an expressed wishing verb. The note does not preserve the uncertainty about the construction and attachment.",
          "suggested_revision": "Document the elliptical greeting interpretation and preserve the alternative source-transparent construction “under the Father of all, through Jesus Christ, always in peace.” Any revised salutation must receive a fresh review.",
          "severity": "minor"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "Ειδως σε, ἀδελφέ μου",
          "english_quote": "Knowing, my brother,",
          "issue": "The supplied “Ειδως” lacks the expected breathing and accent. The intended participle is clear, but this apparent source defect is not disclosed.",
          "suggested_revision": "Record “Ειδως” as supplied and distinguish the expected normalized spelling “Εἰδὼς” from that reading. No change to the meaning of the English is required by this orthographic observation.",
          "severity": "minor"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "τοῖς τὴν καθέδραν αὐτοῦ παρειληφόσιν",
          "english_quote": "who had inherited his seat",
          "issue": "“παρειληφόσιν” expresses having received or taken over something. “Inherited” is less source-transparent and can suggest hereditary or posthumous succession, neither of which this participle specifies.",
          "suggested_revision": "Proposed wording: “who had received his seat.” Preserve the seat image and submit the replacement to fresh review.",
          "severity": "minor"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "τὸν γὰρ αὐτὸν οἱ πανταχῆ ὁμόεθνοι τῆς μοναρχίας καὶ πολιτείας φυλάσσεσι κανόνα, κατὰ μηδένα τρόπον ἄλλως φρονεῖν ὑπὸ τῶν πολλὰ νευεσῶν γραφῶν ἐξοδευθῆναι δυνηθέντες.",
          "english_quote": "Those of their nation everywhere keep the same rule of monarchy and way of life, since in no way have they been able to be diverted into thinking otherwise by scriptures that point in many directions.",
          "issue": "The draft reports “νευεσῶν” but overlooks the apparent malformed finite verb “φυλάσσεσι”. Its English therefore depends on two reconstructed forms, not just the one acknowledged conjecture. Neither reconstruction has been established from the supplied source.",
          "suggested_revision": "Verify both forms against the cited print or other appropriate textual evidence. Distinguish supplied “φυλάσσεσι” from proposed “φυλάσσουσι”, and supplied “νευεσῶν” from proposed “νευουσῶν”. “Keep” and “point in many directions” remain provisional pending that verification and a fresh English review.",
          "severity": "blocking"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "τὰς τῶν προφητῶν πολυσήμες φωνάς",
          "english_quote": "the utterances of the prophets that have many meanings",
          "issue": "The supplied “πολυσήμες” does not provide the expected accusative plural feminine agreement with “φωνάς”. The draft correctly identifies a problem, but its proposed “πολυσήμους” has not thereby become an established source reading.",
          "suggested_revision": "Check the cited print or other appropriate textual evidence for the adjective. Keep supplied “πολυσήμες” distinct from proposed “πολυσήμους”; the rendering “that have many meanings” remains conditional on resolving the defect.",
          "severity": "blocking"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "οὗ ἕνεκεν οὐδενὶ διδάσκειν ἐπιτρέπεσιν , ἐὰν μὴ πρότερον μάθοι",
          "english_quote": "For this reason, they permit no one to teach unless he first learns",
          "issue": "The apparent malformed verb “ἐπιτρέπεσιν” is not identified in the draft's source notes. The English assumes an intelligible third-person plural form without disclosing that reconstruction.",
          "suggested_revision": "Verify the verb against the cited print or other appropriate textual evidence. Record supplied “ἐπιτρέπεσιν” separately from proposed “ἐπιτρέπουσιν”. Re-review the English after the source reading is resolved.",
          "severity": "blocking"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "reference": "2",
      "verdict": "blocked",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": true,
        "no_added_claims": true,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "lexical_fidelity": false,
        "uncertainties_disclosed": false
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "Ἵνα τὸ ὅμοιον καὶ παρ᾽ ἡμῖν γένηται τοῖς ἑβδομήκοντα, ἡμων ἀδελφοῖς τὰς βίβλους μου τῶν κηρυγμάτων δὸς",
          "english_quote": "So that the same may also happen among us, give my books of proclamations to the seventy, our brothers,",
          "issue": "Joining “τοῖς ἑβδομήκοντα” to “ἡμων ἀδελφοῖς” as the recipients of “δὸς” is possible, but it is not established merely by declaring the supplied comma defective. The dative can instead be attached to the preceding purpose/comparison clause, leaving “our brothers” as the recipient phrase. The draft does not preserve that consequential syntactic alternative. The missing accent in “ἡμων” is a separate orthographic matter.",
          "suggested_revision": "Preserve the competing attachments in a translation note and assess the syntax and printed punctuation before fixing the recipients as seventy brothers. Do not classify the comma itself as proven corruption. Distinguish supplied “ἡμων” from expected normalized “ἡμῶν”. Any replacement opening requires fresh review.",
          "severity": "major"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "οὐχ ὡς προφήτης ὥν ἐπίσταμαι",
          "english_quote": "I know this not because I am a prophet,",
          "issue": "The supplied “ὥν” has an anomalous breathing and accent for the participle required by this construction. The draft interprets it as the participle of “εἰμί” without reporting the apparent orthographic defect.",
          "suggested_revision": "Record supplied “ὥν” and identify “ὢν” as the expected normalized form here, not as a verified replacement reading. The English correctly preserves the negation on the prophetic basis of Peter's knowledge.",
          "severity": "minor"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "τὸ δἰ ἐμοῦ νόμιμον",
          "english_quote": "the lawful proclamation made through me",
          "issue": "The supplied “δἰ” uses a breathing-bearing iota where the construction calls for elided “διά”. The draft identifies this correctly, but its note treats this readily intelligible orthographic anomaly as though a substantive textual reconstruction had already been established.",
          "suggested_revision": "Keep supplied “δἰ” distinct from expected normalized “δι᾽”. Describe the anomaly as orthographic unless further evidence shows a substantive defect; do not silently replace the source transcription.",
          "severity": "minor"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "ἐπεχειρησάν τινες",
          "english_quote": "some have attempted",
          "issue": "The supplied verb has only a final accent. In the expected aorist form before enclitic “τινες”, the primary accent on “χεί” should also be present. This apparent accentual defect is not reported.",
          "suggested_revision": "Disclose supplied “ἐπεχειρησάν τινες” separately from the expected normalized sequence “ἐπεχείρησάν τινες”. This observation does not require changing the English meaning.",
          "severity": "minor"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "ἐπχειροῦσιν ἑρμηνεύειν",
          "english_quote": "attempt to interpret",
          "issue": "The supplied “ἐπχειροῦσιν” appears to lack the iota of the prefix in “ἐπιχειροῦσιν”. The draft acknowledges the defect, but acknowledging a proposed correction does not establish that correction or make the translation publishable.",
          "suggested_revision": "Verify the form against the cited print or other appropriate textual evidence. Keep supplied “ἐπχειροῦσιν” distinct from proposed “ἐπιχειροῦσιν”; after verification, review the resulting English afresh.",
          "severity": "blocking"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "reference": "3",
      "verdict": "revise",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": true,
        "no_added_claims": true,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "lexical_fidelity": false,
        "uncertainties_disclosed": false
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "τοῖς τὴν καθέδραν αὐτοῦ παρειληφόσιν",
          "english_quote": "who had inherited his seat",
          "issue": "As in passage 1, “παρειληφόσιν” expresses receipt or taking over. “Inherited” can unnecessarily suggest a hereditary or posthumous mode of succession.",
          "suggested_revision": "Proposed wording: “who had received his seat.” Keep the repeated Greek expression consistent across passages 1 and 3, and review both replacements afresh.",
          "severity": "minor"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "ἵνα οὕτως τὰς πίστεις φυλάξωσιν",
          "english_quote": "so that in this way they may keep their pledges",
          "issue": "“Pledges” is a defensible contextual rendering of plural “πίστεις” and should not automatically be replaced by the policy default “faith”. Nevertheless, the note gives only the rationale for pledges and does not preserve the alternative religious sense of beliefs or commitments of faith. The supplied clause itself does not explicitly identify the content of these “πίστεις”.",
          "suggested_revision": "Retain the plural and disclose the lexical uncertainty: “pledges/assurances” is the preferred contextual interpretation, while “beliefs” or “commitments of faith” remains an alternative. A source-transparent candidate is “so that in this way they may keep the pledges,” accompanied by that note. The candidate is provisional and requires fresh review.",
          "severity": "major"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
